  "title": "HTML Compressor: how it works, formula and examples",
  "excerpt": "HTML Compressor: Minify HTML — strip comments and extra whitespace, keep the page working. Learn how to use it, the formula behind it, a worked example and…",
  "content": "The [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) is a free online tool on CalcHub. Minify HTML — strip comments and extra whitespace, keep the page working. Enter your HTML to compress and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. Because it runs entirely in your browser, the values you type never leave your device, and you can use it as often as you like.\n\nCompress HTML by removing comments and unnecessary whitespace — the spaces, line breaks and indentation that make code readable but that browsers do not need. The compressed page works exactly the same, just with fewer bytes to download. Content inside <pre>, <script> and <style> blocks is left untouched, because whitespace there can change what the page shows or how the code runs. This guide explains how to use it, the formula it applies, and a worked example you can follow with your own numbers.\n\n## How to use the HTML Compressor\n\nStart by typing or pasting your HTML to compress. The result updates as you type, so adjust any value and watch the answer change. If the answer is not what you expected, check the value you entered first — a missing digit or a wrong unit is the usual cause. The steps below walk through each input in order.\n\n1. Paste the HTML you want to compress.\n2. Read the compressed result and the sizes before and after.\n3. Copy the compressed HTML into your page or build step.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) works straight from the values you enter: change an input and the result updates immediately, with no page reload and no waiting. Everything happens in your browser, so your entries never leave your device. Try it with a few different values and you will quickly get a feel for how the inputs move the result.\n\n## Worked example\n\nHere is a typical example. A 179-byte example page with comments and indentation compresses to 126 bytes — about 30% smaller — with the same title, heading and paragraph. Try the same figures in the [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) and you will get the same result — then change one value at a time to see how the answer moves.\n\n## What the result shows\n\nWith the default values (HTML to compress: <!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <!-- Page title --> <title>Hello World</title> </head> <body> <h1>Hello World</h1> <p>Welcome to my page.</p> </body> </html>), the [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) returns Compressed HTML: <!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>Hello World</title></head><body><h1>Hello World</h1><p>Welcome to my page.</p></body></html>, Original size: 179 bytes, Compressed size: 126 bytes, Saved: 29.6%. The first figure is the headline result, and the rest break it down so you can see how the total is made up. If you want to compare with a related calculation, the [Word Counter](/word-counter) is worth a look before you finish.\n\n## What is removed — and what is kept\n\nComments are stripped, whitespace between tags is collapsed, and leading and trailing space on each line is removed. What is never touched: the contents of <pre> blocks (where spaces are part of the content), <script> blocks (where a removed space can break the code) and <style> blocks (where whitespace can matter in selectors and values). Attribute values and text content are preserved exactly.\n\n## Why minify HTML\n\nSmaller pages download faster, which matters most on slow connections and for search rankings — page speed is a ranking factor. The saving on a single small page is modest, but across a whole site, and with the browser caching the result, it adds up. For the biggest wins, minify CSS and JavaScript too, and enable compression on your server.\n\n## Tips\n\nA few things worth knowing before you rely on the result — most of them are about the numbers you put in, rather than the tool itself:\n\n- Keep an uncompressed copy of your source — minified HTML is not meant to be edited.\n- Test the compressed page in a browser before deploying it.\n- Minifying works best combined with gzip or Brotli compression on the server.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Will minifying break my page?\n\nIt should not. Only comments and whitespace between tags are removed, and <pre>, <script> and <style> contents are preserved. Test the result in a browser before deploying, as with any build step. Try it with your own numbers in the [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.\n\n### How much smaller will my HTML get?\n\nTypically 10–30%, depending on how much indentation and commenting the original has. Heavily formatted code compresses the most. Try it with your own numbers in the [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.\n\n### Should I minify HTML by hand or in a build step?\n\nIn a build step, for anything bigger than a single page. This tool is for quick one-off jobs and for checking how much a page can shrink. Try it with your own numbers in the [HTML Compressor](/html-compressor) — the result updates as you type, so you can test a few scenarios in seconds.\n\n### What do I need to use the HTML Compressor?\n\nJust HTML to compress. Enter those values and the result appears straight away — no account or download needed. If you are unsure about a value, try a rough figure first and then refine it — the result updates instantly.\n\n## Related tools\n\nThe [Word Counter](/word-counter), [Character Counter](/character-counter), [Reading Time Calculator](/reading-time-calculator) and [Remove Spaces](/remove-spaces) cover related calculations in the same category. Read the guides to the [Word Counter](/blog/word-counter-guide) and [Character Counter](/blog/character-counter-guide) for more detail on how those work.\n\n## Try it now\n\n- [Open the HTML Compressor](/html-compressor)\n- [Word Counter](/word-counter)\n- [Character Counter](/character-counter)\n- [Reading Time Calculator](/reading-time-calculator)\n- [Remove Spaces](/remove-spaces)\n- [Browse all Text Tools tools](/tools?category=Text%20Tools)\n- [More guides](/blog)",
